Sending a POST request: invoice
URL to send the POST request to: http://localhost:8888/invoice/
Note: format of date of purchase and dates follow ISO 8601 format
The body should contain this JSON object of this format, of this structure and style:
{
    "productPriceIDQuantity": [{
        "product":"64a18e29de2afa121a8db948",
        "price": 123,
        "quantity": 12
    },{
        "product":"64a18e29de2sd",
        "price": 1230,
        "quantity": 12

    },{
        "product":"64a18e29de2afa121asd948",
        "price": 12143,
        "quantity": 12

    }],
    "invoiceID": "INV123",
    "dateOfPurchase": "2013-04-02T14:12:07",
    "supplier": "6498426d9712fa0786e62410",
    "paymentType" : "6491a5d999217ce7abb64a3c",
    "paid": true,
    "GST" : 9,
    "totalPriceBeforeGST": 12,
    "totalPriceAfterGST": 13,
    "currency": "SGD"
}
Take note of the fields - the weird strings are the object's _id (internal MongoDB ID). The object must exist before your POST can be successful!

Sending a POST request: statementOfAccount
URL to send the POST request to: http://localhost:8888/statementOfAccount/
Note: format of date of purchase and dates follow ISO 8601 format
The body should contain this JSON object of this format, of this structure and style:
{
    "referenceNumber": "testReference1234567",
    "invoices": ["64a18f350bb42362532e6bab"],
    "payments": "649e73cee4f474a00c18b84a",
    "dateDue": "2017-04-02T14:12:07",
    "supplier": "6498426d9712fa0786e62410",
    "amountOutstanding": 1,
    "amountPaid": 2,
    "amountOverdue": 3,
    "currency": "THB"
}
Take note of the fields - the weird strings are the object's _id (internal MongoDB ID). The object must exist before your POST can be successful!

Sending a PUT request: invoice
URL to send the PUT request to: http://localhost:8888/invoice/64a18f350bb42362532e6bab
Note: format of date of purchase and dates follow ISO 8601 format
The body should contain this JSON object of this format, of this structure and style:
{
    "totalPriceAfterGST": 1234
}
You need to put in the internal of the object that you want to update in the URL.

For the fields, if it exists, it will update it. If not, it will not update the field
You can also pass in references to other objects with the same format and structure as in the POST request (see the above)

Filtering: Example
For the fields, just follow the MiroBoard class diagram fields
Use camelCase notation. For quantitative fields, you should add min / max as prepend:
Example for field: 'amountPaid':
minAmountPaid = value (NOT IN STRING)
maxAmountPaid = value (NOT IN STRING)

Filtering is mainly for GET requests. So for GET requests to use filtering, follow this structure:

URL: http://localhost:8888/invoice/?productIDPriceQuantityMinPrice=499&productIDPriceQuantityMaxPrice=501
For productIDPriceQuantity field - price: min = 499 and max = 501

For CSV: http://localhost:8888/invoice/csv/?productIDPriceQuantityMinPrice=499&productIDPriceQuantityMaxPrice=501

For non-quantitative fields, just put in as per normal:
URL: http://localhost:8888/statementOfAccount/?currency=THB

TLDR: Quantitative fields: Append with "min" or "max" keyword at the end of the query URL
Then use camelCase notation.

Non-quantitative fields: simply just key = value; currency = THB

Sending a request without adding the JWT Token: Response will look like:

{
    "statusCode": 401,
    "msg": "Invalid Token"
}